                            ===================
                             1. What is GTLib?
                            ===================

GTLib is a library of procedures for OPL-Programmer on the Psion Series 3a.
There are (as the name "Graphic&Tools-Library" indicates) procedures for
graphical purposes but there also exists other useful procedures. For a
short overview of all the functions, have a look at chapter 8.1.



                      ===============================
                       2. Files and its installation
                      ===============================

 gtlib   .opo - \opo\       - *THE* most important :-)
+gtlib   .dbf - \dat\       - DataBaseFile, describes the procedures and
                              is also used by GTHelp
+gthelp  .opo - \opo\       - Describes the procedures and give examples
*gtbuild .mco - \macro\mco\ - Makes programming easier
*gthelp  .opl - \opl\       - Source of GTHelp

+ Not needed for programs which only needs GTLib.opo
* Only interesting for programmers

 gtlib   .doc - this text (the english documentation)
 gtlib   .dok - the german documentation

 gtlib   .cfg - M:\opd\ - created by the GTLib-Menu or -Filerequester, if
                          their settings have been changed

If you don't want to programme and you only "have" to install GTLib, 'cause
another program needs it, the installation is really simple: Just copy
"gtlib.opo" to a \opo\-directory in M:, A: or B: - that's it! GTLib.opo can
also be started on its own (as any other opo-program) - it then displays its
version and how to contact me.

For a closer look, to see all the possibilities of GTLib, you should copy
"gtlib.dbf" in a \dat\-directory and "gthelp.opo" in a \opo\-directory and
run the last one. Press the HELP-Key and you will see a short description,
why gthelp.opo was written and how to use it. (WARNING: This HELP-Function
uses the keyboard-scan-function which does _not_ work on the S3a-Emulator
on a PC => programme seems to 'hang'!)

As a programmer you may also want to have a look at the source of GTHelp
to get some ideas how everything is done. There also exists a macro for
Tom Dolbilins MacroSystem2.0, "gtbuild.mco", which should be located for
example in a \macro\mco\-directory and be installed in the MacSys. Then
it is possible to press its hotkey (assigned in the Macro-Application),
for example Ctrl-Shift-G and then you are able to choose the GTLib-
procedure you want, edit its arguments - and it is in your OPL-code!




           =====================================================
            3. Distribution and notes for shareware-programmers
           =====================================================

GTLib should always be distributed completely; programs which needs GTLib
should have the whole GTLIB.ZIP in its archive:

gtlib.opo  gtlib.dbf  gthelp.opo  gthelp.opl  gtbuild.mco  gtlib.doc  gtlib.dok

The programmer also have to write in his documentation that his program uses
GTLib, that GTLib is _not_ written by him and - especially for shareware and
commercial software - that GTLib is FREEWARE.

For the user and freeware-programmers, GTLib is free of charge.

However if the programmer wants to distribute its software as shareware or
commercial, he has to pay me 3 * the price of his software - or 2 * and
give me a free (registered) copy of his software.

Why? Let me explain: On the one hand I want to support freeware-programmers,
on the other hand I want to avoid that programmers get money for his soft,
which for example consist of 70% GTLib-procedure-calls and so the money
should be mine. If he really has written a goot programme and wants to
distribute it as share or commercial, I won't say anything against, and
I think it is fair that he pays to me 3 * the price of his software (or
2* and a free (registered) copy - surely a small licence-contribution
if he sold 10, 50 or 1000 of his programmes. As he wants his users to pay
for his software it's certainly o.k. for him to pay this little bit to me,
as I also have written a long time on GTLib and its tools. In this case
please contact me and send the money, I will then confirm that I got the
money. The "price" as mentioned above is the price as the software is
sold (incl. tax, handbook and 3.5"-floppy; if it's on SSD, the cost of a SSD
may be substracted). Of course it is forbidden to force the user that he have
to install gtlib.opo twice in a non-\opo\-directory!

Its also strictly forbidden to retranslate gtlib.opo and copy some procedures.

As I can hardly control if there exists a shareware/commercial programme which
uses GTLib and if its programmer has paid for it, I ask every user to contact
me if he finds a shareware/commercial programme which needs this library or
if he finds an archive where GTLib is not completely added to. Just send
a mail to ns103@fim.uni-erlangen.de   - THANKS!

(How to contact me? Have a look at the last chapter!)



                   ====================================
                    4. How to use GTLib (for the user)
                   ====================================

For the user there is not much to say, because the procedures communicates
not very often with the user - only these ones:

a) the new menu-system
b) the filerequester.

Both give hints to its usage in their menus (it's also possible to set their
settings there, espacially the used fonts - this is saved in M:\opo\gtlib.cfg)



                              ==============
                               5. GTLib.dbf
                              ==============

A DataBaseFile which includes each procedures of GTLib and give a short help
about its syntax and arguments. It can be viewed e.g. with the DATAApplication
or - better - with GTHelp.opo.



                              ===============
                               6. GTHelp.opo
                              ===============

An example-programme with its source included in this GTLib-archive. It needs
the file "GTLib.dbf" in one of the \dat\-directory on M:, A: or B:. You choose
the procedure you want to have help for with the cursor-keys and press RETURN.
Then you'll see in three windows the syntax, the description and the used
arguments/variables - and if you press the s-key you'll get a small example
of this procedure! And because the source is also be distributed, it is
a good idea for programmers which want to know more how to write programmes
with GTLib to have a look at this source.



                             ================
                              7. GTBuild.mco
                             ================

GTBuild.mco is a macro for Tom Dolbilins MacroSystem v2.0 which makes it
easy to insert the GTLib-Procedures in your OPL-source without to look at
the documentation always. As soon as it is installed in MacroSystem
(MacSys/"Macro") and assigned with a hotkey, the macro is started with
its hotkey. GTBuild opens a dialog on the OPL-editor-screen and asks to
choose if you want to insert a graphical or a non-grafical procedure
or if you want to insert the "start-procedure". In the first two cases
there will a second dialog be opend in which you choose the procedure.
Then every argument of this procedure will be asked - there you can type
in a value or the name of a variable. You will also get a small description
of every procedure. If you press ESCape on the last argument or the dialog
returns with an empty input, it is aborted. Otherwise the whole GTLib-
procedure-call will be inserted in your OPL-source.

Example: We want to delete every space from the string "Line1". As the
function "trim$:()" would not delete the spaces in the middle of the string,
we have to use exc$:(). So we press the GTBuild.mco-hotkey, choose "GTLib-
other functions" and there "$-Exchange". Then the Syntax will be displayd
and you are asked to type in the first argument, the variable-name from
the string we want to exchange something. In our case it would be: Line1$
and we press RETURN. Then "org$" will be asked, meaning the string the
procedure will look for and be replaced. As we know it, we can type " "
incl. the "s. In the last dialog we will be asked with what we want to
replace the " "s - here we type in an empty string: ""  incl. "s! And
with the next press of RETURN we will see the following inserted on the
cursorposition:   exc$:(line1$," ","")    .Of course we want to use
the returned string of the procedure, we have to write a   line1$=   in
front of it. This will be not asked by GTBuild because such a returned
value could also be an argument of another command (e.g. PRINT)!



   ====================================================================
    8. Overview and description of the 39 procedures (for programmers)
   ====================================================================

8.1.1 Overview of all procedures - the graphical procedures:
------------------------------------------------------------
gplot:    - Draws a pixel
gline:    - Draws a linie
gbox:     - Draws a box
gbbev:    - Draws a 'bevel'-border
gbdbl:    - Draws a 'double-bevel'(embossed)-border
gcircle:  - Draws a circle
gpfill:   - Draws a filled box
gatcpr:   - Makes a centered 'gAT'
ggprint:  - Prints a text in grey
gsprint:  - Prints a text with shadow
goprint:  - Prints a text outlined/3D-like
gwprint:  - Prints a text in a box + Wordwrap
gedwl%:   - To determine how much Text could be ..
gedwf%:   - .. printed in a box
gedit$:   - To edit text
gsbar:    - Draws a scroll-bar/area
gmsbar:   - Moves a scroll-bar
getcont%: - Returns the screen-contrast
setcont:  - Sets the screen-contrast
expwino:  - Opens an 'exploding window'
expwinc:  - Closes an 'exploding window' 

8.1.2 Overview of all procedures - the other procedures:
--------------------------------------------------------
gtver:    - Checks the version of the installed GTLib
rand:     - Makes a "Super-Randomize"
bin$:     - Converts a Byte to a binary string
bin2dec&: - Converts a binary string to long integer
trim$:    - Deletes Spaces at the beginning/end(/mid) of a string
insert$:  - Inserts something in a string
delete$:  - Deletes something from a string
midx$:    - Overwrites something in a string
exc$:     - Search&Replace something in a string
rloc%:    - Locates the last occurence of a string in a string
scan%:    - Checks if a special key is pressed
fisr$:    - Searches for a file on M: A: and B:
freq$:    - Opens a file- or font-requester to choose a file/font
readdir%: - Reads a directory in memory
sort:     - Sorts a directory (or similar) in memory
nminit%:  - Initialize a 'NMenu' - a new menu-replace
nmcard:   - Adds a menu to a NMenu
nmenu%:   - Calls the 'NMenu' (Display-Choose-Return)

8.1.3 Other internal procedures which should not be used by the programmer
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
gtlib:    - 1. Procedure, used if GTLib is started with "RunOPL"
nmenucfg: - Used by NMenu to display the help and edit the configuration
frqmain:  - Used by the FileRequester
frqmen:   - Used by the FileRequester
frqdvi:   - Used by the FileRequester
edflist:  - Used by the FileRequester
eddlist:  - Used by the FileRequester
gtrconf$: - Used by NMenu & FileReq. to read their configuration
gtwconf:  - Used by NMenu & FileReq. to write their configuration


8.2. How to use GTLib (in general)
----------------------------------
GTLib.opo is located in a \opo\-directory on M: A: or B:. So Every programmer
has to check before a LOADM on which drive GTLib is. For an example have a
look at GTHelp.opl or use gtbuild.mco.


8.3. Description of each procedure, sorted alphabetically
---------------------------------------------------------

Colors available at col% etc.: 0=black 1=grey 2=white
GraphicModes available at gmode% etc.: 0=set 1=erase 2=invert 3=replace


bin$= bin$:(byte%)
      -----
     Converts the byte byte% in a 8-chars-long binary string
     ( Example: bin$:(141)="10001101" )



dec&= bin2dec&:(bin$)
      ---------
     Converts a binary string to a long integer
     ( Example: bin2dec&:("1101") = 13 )



new$= delete$:(org$,pos%,len%)
      --------
     Deletes len% chars from the string org$ at position pos%
     Ex.: delete$:("123456",3,2) returns "1256"



new$= exc$:(text$,org$,repl$)
      -----
     Searches for the string org$ in the string text$ and replaces every
     occurence with repl$
     Ex.: exc$:("-1-2-3-","-","..") returns "..1..2..3.."



expwinc:(x%,y%,width%,height%)
--------
        ExplodingWindows - Effect, this procedure should be called after
        closing a window.



expwino:(x%,y%,width%,height%)
--------
        ExplodingWindows - Effect, this is the opening-procedure meaning that
        the window "explodes" from the middle of the screen to its position
        x%,y% and its width widht% and height height%. There will be _no_
        window or bitmap created! The Effect is also be drawn in the actual
        window so it may not look as good if there already exists "real"
        windows on the screen or if the actual is not the background one etc.



file$= fisr$:(file$,mode%)
       ------
     Searches for the file file$ on M: A: or B: and returns the complete
     filename incl. M: A: or B: or "" if nothing found. If mode%=1 and
     nothing is found, a (standard) filerequester will be opend.
     Ex.: fisr$:("\opo\gtlib.opo") may return "M:\opo\gtlib.opo"



file$= freq$:(title$,file$,mode%)
       ------
     Opens and displays a file-requester with the title title$ and the given
     filename file$. Returns "" if the filereq. has been canceld or returns
     the complete filename incl. path etc.
     The user is able to choose the fonts used by the filereq.
     mode%=0 opens the "normal" filereq, mode%=2 opens a font-file-requester
     which displays every font found in \fon\ on M: A: and B:.
     If mode%=1 it should be able to select more than one file ("Multi-File-
     Requester"), but this isn't included in the GTLib-Version 1.0 yet ;-)



gatcpr:(text$,x1%,y1%,x2%,y2%)
-------
       Makes a centered 'gAT', this means if you print text$ with gPRINT etc.
       it is centered between x1%,y1% and x2%,y2%



gbbev:(x%,y%,width%,height%,state%)
------
      Draws a 'bevel'-border (top&left side of box white, bottom&right side
      black if state%=0 (=raised), or vice versa if state%=1 (=sunken)



gbdbl:(x%,y%,width%,height%,state%)
------
      Draws a 'double-bevel'(embossed)-border, that means a bevel-border
      around a bevel-border with the reversed state%. If state%=0 the effect
      is sunken, if state%=1 the effect is raised.



gbox:(x%,y%,width%,height%,col%,gmode%)
-----
     Draws a box at x%,y% with the width width% and height height% in color
     col% and mode gmode%



gcircle:(x%,y%,radius%,fill%,col%,gmode%)
--------
        Draws a circle at x%,y% with the radius radius%, color col% and
        mode gmode%. If fill%=1 the circle will be filled. You should not
        combine fill%=1 and gmode%=2 together, 'cause you won't get a filled
        inverted circle but a little bit garbage ;-)



text$= gedit$:(text$,addr(e%()))
       -------
      Edits the text text$. e%() is an array with at least 16 elements
      which have the following meaning:
      e%(1) = The character-position of the textcursor in the text
      e%(2) = X-Position in pixels of the textcursor in the text; if it's
              < as the start of the edit-box, the cursor will be set to
              the beginning of the text, if it's > as the end, cursor is
              after the last char in the text, otherwise it's in the mid.
      e%(3) = Maximum length of text$
      e%(4) = Start of edit-box (X-coordinate, in pixels)
      e%(5) = End of edit-box (X-coordinate, in pixels)
      e%(6) = Way of input (0=from the left, reserved for future versions,
              in which 1 may be centered and 3=from the left)
      e%(7) = Allowed input; 0=all chars allowed, 1=only numbers 1234567890
              and ,.   2=text will be only printed, no edit allowed
      e%(8) = Color of the text (as every col%)
      e%(9) = Background-color ('')
      e%(10)= ID of background-pattern (look at gpfill for more informations)
      e%(11)= Color of background-pattern ('')
      e%(12)= Sets a left marker (X-coordinate in pixels); if the cursor passes
              this mark to the left, gedit$: returns it in e%(16);
              if e%(12)=-1 this will be disabled.
      e%(13)= Similar to e%(13) but a right marker and gedit$ returns when
              the cursor passes the mark to the right.
      e%(14)= returns the code of the last key pressed
      e%(15)= returns the "kmod" of the last key pressed
      e%(16)= returns -1 if the cursor passed the left or +1 if the cursor
              passed the right mark.

      gedit$: will always return if a key is pressed which is not a letter
      or a number: RETURN, TAB, UP&DOWN, CTRL/PSION+key or the cursor-key
      left&right if it is pressed at the start/end of the text, etc. Or
      if the left or right mark (e%(12) / e%(13)) has been passed. This is
      why gedit$: always have to be within a loop!
      Example:
                 do
                  text$=gedit$:(text$,addr(e%()))
                  k%=e%(14)
                 until k%=9 or k%=13 or k%=27 :rem edit until ESC/TAB/RETURN
                 if k%=9 or k%=13 :rem TAB/RETURN -> e.g. to the next field
                 elseif k%=27 :rem ESCAPE, e.g. to abort
                 endif



fpos%= gedwf%:(text$,lpos%,maxlen%)
       -------
      Checks how much text could be printed in a maxlen% width box (in pixels),
      if lpos% is the position (in chars) of the char in the text which should
      be the last printed. Returns the first position fpos% of the text from
      which the text could be printed in the maxlen%-width box if fpos% is the
      first and lpos% the last character of text$.
      ('gtwidth' of text$ between fpos% and lpos% <= maxlen% pixel)
      [ In the german-doc., it was not easier for me to describe this, I know
      it's hardly to understand... ;-) ]



lpos%= gedwl%:(text$,fpos%,maxlen%)
       -------
      Checks how much text could be printed in a maxlen% width box (in pixels),
      if fpos% is the position (in chars) of the char in the text which should
      be the first printed. Returns the last position lpos% of the text from
      which the text could be printed in the maxlen%-width-box if lpos% is the
      last and fpos% the first character of text$.
      ('gtwidth' of text$ between fpos% and lpos% <= maxlen% pixel)
      [ In the german-doc., it was not easier for me to describe this, I know
      it's hardly to understand... ;-) ]



contrast= getcont%: 
          ---------
         Returns the screen-contrast, 0=light, 15=dark



ggprint:(text$)
--------
        Prints the text text$ in grey - this means that the text will be erased
        in the black bitplane and set in the grey one.



gline:(x1%,y1%,x2%,y2%,col%,gmode%)
------
      Draws a line from x1%,y1% to x2%,y2% in color col% and mode gmode%



gmsbar:(addr(s%()),news5)
-------
       Moves a previous with gmsbar:() drawn SBar, news5 is the new value of
       s%(5) which indicates the start of the SBar in promille (0-999). At
       the END of this procedure the value of news5 will be saved in s%(5),
       this means that you may not do this before, it's done automatically!



goprint:(text$,col1%,col2%,col3%)
--------
        Prints the text text$ outline / 3d-like; col1% is the left/top color,
        col2% the inside-color, col3% the bottom/right color. 3D-raised would
        be for example ...text$,2,1,0)



gpfill:(x%,y%,w%,h%,col%,mod%,p_id%,p_col%,p_mod%)
-------
       Draws a filled area/box at x%,y% with the width w% and height h%, color
       col% and mode gmode%. The box will also be filled with a pattern, if
       p_id%<>0; p_id% is then the pattern-id (-1 is a greyish raster), p_col%
       the color of the pattern and p_mod% the mode of the pattern. With the
       -1 pattern it is easy to create 2 additional grey-colors:
       gpfill:(x%,y%,w%,h%,0,3,0,0,0) - black & no Pattern = black
       gpfill:(x%,y%,w%,h%,1,3,-1,0,0)- grey & black Raster = dark-grey
       gpfill:(x%,y%,w%,h%,1,3,0,0,0) - grey & no Pattern = medium-grey
       gpfill:(x%,y%,w%,h%,1,3,-1,2,0)- grey & white Raster = light-grey
       gpfill:(x%,y%,w%,h%,2,3,0,0,0) - white & no Pattern = white



gplot:(x%,y%,col%,gmode%)
------
      Draws a pixel at x%,y% in color col% and mode gmode%



gsbar:(addr(s%()))
------
      Draws a scroll-bar, s%() is an array with at least 16 elements:
      s%(1) = X-coordinate (top left) of the start of the SBar
      s%(2) = Y-coordinate (top left) of the start of the SBar
      s%(3) = Width of the SBar in pixel
      s%(4) = Height of the SBar in pixel
      s%(5) = Start of the SBar in promille (1/1000): 0-999
      s%(6) = End of the SBar in promille (1/1000): 1-1000
      s%(7) = Orientation of the SBar, 0=horizontal 1=vertical
      s%(8) = Background-color (as used in col%)
      s%(9) = ID of the background-pattern (similar to gpfill: !)
      s%(10)= Color of the background-pattern ('')
      s%(11)= Color of the SBar
      s%(12)= ID of the SBar-Pattern (similar to gpfill: !)
      s%(13)= Color of the SBar-Pattern ('')
      s%(14)= Design of the SBar (0=normal, 1=shadowed, 2=with bevel-border,
              3=with reverse bevel-border, 4="goonie" (bevel&middle-knob)
      s%(15)= returns the top (if SBar is vertical) or left (of SBar is
              horizontal) coordinate of the SBar
      s%(16)= returns the length of the SBar in pixels, the length in X
              if its horizontal or in Y if its vertical

      A SBar has two different usages: on the one hand you can display
      how much % of something is done. The start is always the same then
      and the end is increased from 1 to 1000. On the other hand it could
      be used as a position-display with variable starts, an example is
      here the SBars used in the filerequester.



gsprint:(text$,scol%,tcol%,dir%)
--------
        Prints the text text$ in color tcol% shadowed with color scol%.
        This means,  the Text is first written in scol% and then in col%.
        dir% is the direction of the shadow as a          7 8 9  \ | /
        numeric keypad with '5' as the text.              4 T 6  - T -
        A "normal" shadow would be: ..text$,0,2,3         1 2 3  / | \



ver= gtver:(min)
     ------
     Checks the version of the installed, loaded GTLib. If this version is
     >=min, the actual version-number will be returned. If the version is
     <min, an alert will occur with the note, the user needs at least the
     version min of GTLib if he want to use this program, which will then be
     stoped.



[lines%=] gwprint:(text$,width%,maxlines%,mode%,ysp%)
          --------
         Prints the text text$ at the actual cursor-position without getting
         over the width width%. If the text is larger, it will be continued
         in the next line. This line-break will be wordwrapped. The space
         between each line could be make bigger with ysp%>0 or smaller with
         ysp%<0. It returns the number of lines printed and set the graphic-
         cursor to the next line so that it is possible to make the next
         gwprint:. Maximal maxlines% lines will be printed, if maxlines%<0
         only this one line will be printed.
         mode%: 0=text will be left-alligned
                1=text will be right-allignes
                2=text will be centered
                3=text will be left&right-alligned
                4=no print, only calculate & return the nr. of lines needed
         Examples: - ..(text$,240,3,2,1) - prints the text in a 240 pixel width
                     box, text is left&right-alligned, max. 3 lines will be
                     printed and 1 pixel added between each line
                     to the height of the font
                   - ..(text$,240,-2,2,0) - only the second line will be
                     printed, and this will be centerd.



new$= insert$:(org$,ins$,pos%)
      --------
     Inserts the string ins$ into the string org$ at position pos%
     Ex.: insert$:("123456","..",4) returns "123..456"



new$= midx$:(org$,ins$,pos%)
      ------
     Overwrites the string ins$ in the string org$ at position pos%
     Ex.: midx$:("123456","..",4) returns "123..6"



ad%= nminit%:(men%,maxentries%,maxlength%)
     --------
    Initialize a NMenu. NMenu is a replacement for the Psion/OPL-Menu -
    it looks better and gives the User the chance to define 3 fonts in
    different sizes which will be automatically zoomed with the number
    of entries in a menu (the more entries the smaller the font).
    Additionaly there may exists menu-entries without a Psion-Hotkey!
    And the feature that menus, even too big to display with the smallest
    font, will be scrolled! So it's possible for example to have 50
    Entries under one menu! The next advantage is, that NMenu can be called
    more times without re-initialize it as you have to do it with the
    build-in menu! Of course you could then have more, different menus which
    you could initialize at the start of your program. There are only two
    disadvantages: The speed, drawing takes a little bit longer, and that
    it needs 2 Windows, so that your programme may only use 5 Windows/Bitmaps
    (2 for NMenu and 1 for the Filereq.!)
    men% is the number of "menu-cards". If a NMenu is displayd, you will see
     one card more (this is NMenu's menu for its help and configuration)
     You _MUST_ give here the correct value, for every men% you must have
     a nmcard:-call later. Otherwise you may get an error when you call the
     menu and NMenu's menu couldn't be used correctly.
    maxentries% is the max. number of entries in a menu-card. If <4 it will
     be 4, 'cause NMenu's menu has 4 entries.
    maxlength% is the max. length of a entry including its PSION-Hotkey. If
     <12 it will be 12, 'cause of NMenu's menu.
    Returns the address ad% where NMenu is located in memory. Must be passed
    to the other nm-procedures. Will be 0 if not enough memory free.


nmcard:(ad%,title$,entries$)
-------
       Adds a new menu (menu-card) to the NMenu at address ad% (will be
       returned by nminit%: !). It is totally necessary to make as many
       nmcard:-calls as you defined with men% in nminit%: !
       title$ is the title of the new menu,
       entries$ - all entries of the menu with their PSION-Hotkey in the
       following format: "aEntry1,bEntry2,.." whereas a and b are the PSION-
       Hotkeys. If the entry should have no hotkey, use a space instead.
       If a line should be drawn, use "%-" instead of Entry&Hotkey.
       Example: "lLoad,sSave,%- Quit", whereas Quit will have no hotkey and
       a line between Save and Quit.


r%=nmenu%:(ad%)
   -------
       Adds the NMenu-Menu to the NMenu at address ad% and display it.
       The user can choose an entry as he is used to do. It returns r%
       which is >512 if the entry has a Psion-Hotkey (512+asc(hotkey)),
       =0 if ESC was pressed or between 1 and 512 if the entry has no
       hotkey and the selected menu is menu_card%*max_entries%+selected_entry%.
       So you cannot have unlimited menus, men%*maxentries% should be <512...
       As said, the NMenu at address ad% will not be deleted so you can call
       nmenu%: again and again. Of course NMenu remembers the choosen entry
       and displays it on the next NMenu-call. If a menu isn't needed any
       longer, free the memory with "FREEALLOC ad%".



rand:    Makes a "Super"-'Randomize' and is the "Shortform" for
-----     "randomize datetosecs(year,month,day,hour,minute,second)"



ad%= readdir%:(path$,format%,space%)
     ---------
    Allocates a heap-cell as done with "ALLOC" and reads a directory in it.
    It returns the address ad% which is the start of the cell. path$ is
    the path to be read in. ad%=0 if memory full.
    format%: 0=filenames will be formatted (8+"."+3, e.g. "GTLIB   .OPO")
             1=don't format filenames, filled with spaces to length=12
               (e.g. "GTLIB.OPO   ")
             2=also read length of file (4 bytes, LongInteger)
             4=add file-attributes (2 bytes, Integer(Word))
             8=add Time&Date (4 bytes, LongInteger)
            16=Replace the extension with the Drive-Letter as the FontFileReq.
               will do it (e.g. "GTLIB    [M]")
            ^^- may be combined (add together)
    space% is the space between each entry in the memory (e.g. to mark a file)
    peekw(ad%) = Number of files read in (Integer/Word)
    peek$(ad%+2) = the first file, always 12 chars long. If the filesize is
    also read in, you can get the size of the first file with peekl(ad%+2+13).
    If a file is not a file but a directory, its size will be set to -1 (So
    you have to read the size in if you want to check if a file is a directory
    (or you read the attributes). If attributes and/or time&date are read in,
    it is in the same order as format%.
    The attributes, as described in the Psionics-SysCalls.1-File:
      Bit 0: file is not ReadOnly     Bit 1: file is Hidden
      Bit 2: file is a System file    Bit 3: file is a Volume name
      Bit 4: file is a Directory      Bit 5: file is Modified
      Bit 8: file is a readable file  Bit 9: file is an executable file
      Bit 11: file is a text file     Bit 10: file is a byte stream file

    Example: format%=2 - FileNames will be formatted and the filesize will
    be read. space%=1. So the length of each entry is: 13 (the name) + 4
    (the length) + 1 (space%) = 18. The Number of entries at ad% is 2 bytes
    long, so the start of each entry is: ad%+2+18*x%, whereas x% is the
    number of the files between 0 and peekw(ad%)-1. peek$(ad%+2+18*2) will
    so return the name of the third file.



pos%= rloc%:(a$,b$)
      ------
     Returns the last occurence of the string b$ in the string a$ (reverse LOC)
     Ex.: rloc%:("1.3.5.7",".") returns 6



k%= scan%:(key$)
    ------
    Checks, if the key key$ is pressed. key$ is either A-Z, 1-0, / * + - , or .
    whereas a-z (small letters) are _not_ the same as A-Z; a-z could be used
    instead of the following keywords:
      SYSTEM a     CALC   g     ENTER   l     RSHIFT  p     DOWN  v
      DATA   b     SHEET  h     RETURN  l     PSION   q     RIGHT w
      WORD   c     ESC    i     CONTROL m     MENU    r
      AGENDA d     DELETE j     CTRL    m     DIAMOND s
      TIME   e     DEL    j     LSHIFT  n     HELP    t
      WORLD  f     TAB    k     UP      o     LEFT    u

    If the checked key is pressed, k%=-1 will be returned.
    This function uses a OS-Call which will not work on the S3a-Emulator on a
    PC. Then the procedure always returns 0. This is, as said, a limitation
    of the emulator.
    Differences between german and english keyboard-layout are corrected.


setcont:(contrast%)
--------
        Sets the screen-contrast to contrast% (0=light ..to.. 15=dark)



sort:(ad%,size%,sort%)
-----
     Sorts a directory at address ad% which was read in with readdir%.
     Theoretically you could also use this procedure to sort other datas,
     if you write it in the memory as readdir%: would do it. size% is
     the size of each entry (look at readdir% how to calculate it).
     sort%: 1=sort entries alphabetically
            2=Directories before files (note: see next line)
            4=Directories after files (note: sort will check a file as a
              directory only if the filesizes are also read, a entry is
              a directory if the filesize is -1 !)
            8=filesize (small files first)
           16=extension (alphabetically)
           ^^- can be combined (add together!)



text$= trim$:(text$,mid%)
       ------
     Deletes spaces at the start/end of the string text$. If mid%=1 the
     spaces in the mid will be reduced to one any time.
     Ex.: trim$:(" One   Test  ",1) returns "One Test"






                         =========================
                          9. Bugs and Limitations
                         =========================

scan: uses an OS-Call which doesn't work on the S3a-Emulator on a PC. So it is
 not able to check if a key is pressed on the emulator.
 On not-english and not-german keyboard, the scan-function may be wrong, 'cause
 other keyboard-layouts are not supported yet in my scan:-function. If the
 keyboard is not a german one, it is supposed to be a english. This is because
 the scan-call doesn't check if the key X is pressed, but if the 'X key in the
 Y row is pressed'...
 
gcircle: It is impossible yet to draw a filled inverted circle because of the
 way this procedure works.

gmsbar: It seems that the bar is sometimes printed on pixel too large, I've
 seen it in the filerequester by scrolling around that once there was no
 space between the bar and the border, but I've no idea why. This will not
 happen very often however I think (and hope ;) )

freq/menu: The filereq. needs a window. NMenu needs two. As it is possible to
 have both their windows open, there will be max. 3 windows used. In OPL you
 can have upto 8 Bitmap/WindowIDs, so you have only 5 left for your own use..




